#N canvas 20 20 3600 2500 12;
#X text 20 20 INQUIRY PROBE -- does a device answer a universal device inquiry. Send F0 7E 7F 06 01 F7 to one device at a time and watch sysexin. The hot-swap detector branched on the answer: a device that REPLIES can be actively polled -- are you still there -- and one that does not can only be watched passively \, by when it last spoke. ✅ ANSWERED \, item 249: all three reply and byte 5 tells them apart \, so all three are polled. See ref/module/presence.md., f 96;
#X text 20 140 ⛔ IT ASKS THE LAUNCHPAD FIRST AND THAT IS THE WHOLE METHOD. The Launchpad is KNOWN to answer \, in either mode -- so phase 1 proves the CHANNEL works. A silence from the nano or the 404 means nothing until phase 1 has answered in the SAME RUN. Prove the probe before believing the silence., f 96;
#X text 20 250 IT RECORDS ITSELF BECAUSE THERE IS NO CONSOLE. A menu-launched patch runs -nogui and stdout goes to tty1 \, which VNC will not show -- so every line goes to /sdcard/inquiry-probe.log and is read back over ssh afterwards. Same reasoning as u_err's persistent log and AP Probe's., f 96;
#X text 20 360 ⚠️ NOT DEPLOYED WITH Cut It. It is a MENU patch \, so running it costs no killall pd -- which strands the Launchpad in Programmer Mode every time. Copy the folder to /sdcard/Patches/! debug/ and select it. 📄 The 404's implementation chart marks SysEx x in BOTH directions \, so expect a no there -- this measures whether the documentation is right., f 96;
#X obj 1340 1160 s screenLine2;
#X msg 1400 1450 240 \, 126 \, 127 \, 6 \, 1 \, 247;
#X obj 1400 1520 midiout;
#X text 1620 1520 ONE midiout \, and THE PORT IS SET COLD IMMEDIATELY BEFORE EACH SEND. Pd 0.49 does not warn about a wrong or missing port at all \, so if nothing answers anywhere \, suspect this before suspecting the bytes., f 48;
#X obj 700 1800 list append;
#X obj 700 1850 t l b;
#X obj 1000 1900 text size \$0-log;
#X obj 700 1960 text set \$0-log;
#X obj 20 2060 text define \$0-log;
#X msg 20 2000 write -c /sdcard/inquiry-probe.log;
#X text 1300 1800 THE LOG APPEND IS u_err's AND State Probe's \, trap and all. text set wants a LIST and every line here arrives with its first word as the SELECTOR \, so list append is load-bearing -- without it Pd answers no method for ASK. text size gives the next line number and text set PAST THE END APPENDS \, which is how the log grows with nothing tracking its length., f 60;
#X obj 20 520 loadbang;
#X obj 20 570 del 1500;
#X obj 20 620 t b b b b b;
#X text 240 520 ⚠️ loadbang FIRES BEFORE ALSA CONNECTIONS EXIST. Anything sent on loadbang itself goes nowhere at all., f 44;
#X msg 900 690 sh inquiry-wire.sh;
#X obj 900 740 shell;
#X obj 900 790 t a a;
#X obj 1140 840 print WIRE;
#X obj 900 840 s screenLine4;
#X text 900 900 ⛔ THE COMMAND LIVES IN A SCRIPT \, NOT IN A MESSAGE BOX. Pd atomises a message on spaces and shell rejoins it \, so quoting and redirection do not survive the trip., f 44;
#X msg 1500 690 symbol INQUIRY-PROBE;
#X obj 1500 740 s screenLine1;
#X msg 1800 690 symbol see-sdcard-log;
#X obj 1800 740 s screenLine5;
#X msg 600 690 BOOT inquiry-probe;
#X obj 20 690 del 2000;
#X obj 20 740 t b b;
#X msg 220 790 1;
#X obj 20 800 metro 4000;
#X obj 20 860 f;
#X obj 240 920 + 1;
#X obj 20 920 t f f f;
#X obj 20 980 select 1 2 3 4;
#X text 420 800 ONE PHASE EVERY FOUR SECONDS \, and the four seconds are for the HUMAN rather than the device -- a reply arrives in milliseconds. It is long enough to read the screen and see which device is being asked., f 44;
#X obj 20 1050 t b b b b;
#X msg 320 1110 1;
#X msg 220 1170 symbol ask-launchpad;
#X msg 120 1230 ASK 1 launchpad;
#X obj 440 1050 t b b b b;
#X msg 740 1110 2;
#X msg 640 1170 symbol ask-nanokontrol;
#X msg 540 1230 ASK 2 nanokontrol;
#X obj 860 1050 t b b b b;
#X msg 1160 1110 3;
#X msg 1060 1170 symbol ask-sp404;
#X msg 960 1230 ASK 3 sp404;
#X obj 1340 1050 t b b b;
#X msg 1340 1110 stop;
#X msg 1560 1230 END probe-complete;
#X msg 1340 1290 symbol DONE-read-the-log;
#X obj 1780 1290 del 200;
#X text 1780 1350 THE FILE IS WRITTEN 200 ms AFTER THE END LINE \, not with it. text set is synchronous but the write has to see the finished text \, and a del costs nothing here., f 44;
#X obj 2300 700 sysexin;
#X obj 2300 760 t f f;
#X obj 2300 820 pack f f;
#X obj 2300 880 list prepend RX;
#X obj 2300 940 list trim;
#X obj 2300 1000 t a a;
#X obj 2560 1060 print RX;
#X text 2300 1120 ⚠️ sysexin IS OMNI AND CANNOT SAY WHO REPLIED \, which is exactly why the phases are asked ONE AT A TIME. The phase is packed alongside every byte \, so RX 240 1 is the Launchpad and RX 240 2 would be the nano. sysexin has a second outlet carrying a port \, but nothing in this project has ever used it -- treat it as unverified rather than building on it., f 56;
#X obj 3000 820 f;
#X obj 3000 880 t f f;
#X obj 3240 940 + 1;
#X obj 3000 940 makefilename rx-%d;
#X obj 3000 1000 s screenLine3;
#X text 3000 1060 A RUNNING BYTE COUNT IS THE BINARY READOUT. Nobody has to read or remember a reply -- only whether the number MOVES while a given device is being asked., f 44;
#X text 420 940 ⛔ THE COUNTER SEEDS 1 \, NOT 0. metro fires the INSTANT it starts and f is tapped BEFORE the + 1 stores \, so a seed of 0 spends the whole first tick on a phase that matches nothing -- every device then gets asked four seconds late and anything arriving in that dead window is logged as phase 0. Found by running the probe on the Mac against a fake device rather than by reading it, f 44;
